    "question": {
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=-4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $-4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=-3.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $-3$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x-3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}-4xy=-3y$ only.",
